# Metadata Model

Each file should include

- File ID
- Original filename
- Stored filename
- Owner ID
- Organization ID
- MIME type
- Size
- Checksum
- Upload timestamp
- Last modified
- Status

---

# File Naming

Uploaded filenames should never be stored as the physical filename.

Generate unique identifiers.

Example

```text
f_93b7f8c29d41.pdf

img_8f7bc92a.png

report_5dca1234.xlsx
```

This prevents collisions and improves security.

---

# Directory Structure

Logical organization

```text
organizations/

users/

reports/

avatars/

exports/

documents/
```

Storage providers may implement equivalent bucket structures.

---

# Maximum File Sizes

Recommended defaults

| File Type | Maximum Size |
|-----------|--------------|
| Avatar | 5 MB |
| Document | 25 MB |
| Report | 50 MB |
| Export | 100 MB |

Limits may be configured by administrators.

---

# Validation

Validate

- MIME type
- File extension
- File size
- Ownership
- Authorization

Reject unsupported uploads before storage.

---

# Security

Uploaded files should

- Be authenticated
- Be authorized
- Be virus scanned (future)
- Use randomized filenames
- Never execute as server-side code

Executable uploads should be blocked by default.

---

# Access Control

Files belong to

- Individual users
- Organizations
- System resources

Access decisions should follow the platform authorization model.

---

# Versioning

Future support may include

- File version history
- Version rollback
- Change tracking
- Previous revisions

Current implementation stores only the latest version.

---

# Deletion Strategy

Deletion should follow a soft-delete workflow.

```text
Database

↓

Mark Deleted

↓

Retention Period

↓

Permanent Removal

↓

Storage Cleanup
```

This supports recovery and auditing.

---

# Backup Strategy

Back up independently

- PostgreSQL metadata
- Object storage

Both backups should be restorable independently.

---

# Performance Goals

| Metric | Target |
|---------|---------|
| Upload Start | < 200 ms |
| Metadata Save | < 100 ms |
| Download Initialization | < 150 ms |

Large files should support streaming where appropriate.

---

# Monitoring

Track

- Upload count
- Download count
- Failed uploads
- Failed downloads
- Storage utilization
- Average file size
- Upload latency

---

# Storage Providers

Development

- Local Docker volume

Production options

- Amazon S3
- Cloudflare R2
- Google Cloud Storage
- Azure Blob Storage
- MinIO (Self-hosted)

The application should remain storage-provider agnostic.

---

# Alternatives Considered

## Database BLOB Storage

Pros

- Simple querying
- Single backup

Cons

- Large database size
- Slower performance
- Poor scalability

Decision

Rejected.

---

## Local Server Filesystem

Pros

- Easy setup

Cons

- Difficult scaling
- Poor redundancy
- Complicated backups

Decision

Accepted for local development only.

---

## Object Storage

Pros

- Highly scalable
- Cost effective
- Reliable
- CDN compatible
- Cloud native

Decision

Accepted.

---

# Consequences

Positive

- Smaller databases
- Faster backups
- Better scalability
- Easier CDN integration
- Independent storage scaling

Negative

- Additional infrastructure
- Separate backup strategy
- More deployment configuration

---

# Future Evolution

Future enhancements include

- CDN integration
- Image optimization
- Automatic thumbnail generation
- AI content analysis
- File deduplication
- Encrypted storage
- Multi-region replication
- Lifecycle policies
- Signed download URLs

---

# Decision Summary

R-zension adopts a hybrid file storage architecture where PostgreSQL stores metadata while binary content is stored in dedicated object storage. This design improves scalability, security, maintainability, and performance while remaining independent of any specific storage provider.

